Scenario Going to a transaction opens its register at that line
Description Which register: the one of the real account the transaction
Description touches. A category has none to browse, and the bank side is where
Description the figure can be checked against a statement and corrected.
Details The line is selected, and the window scrolls it into view. Twenty
Details years of history is tens of thousands of lines, so a selection nobody
Details can see is the same as none.
Details
Details Named by its ref here because a table has to name it somehow. What the
Details report hands over is the transaction's own id, which is why the id is
Details on both pages; see the scenario below.
Details
Details A transfer touches two real accounts and so has two registers it could
Details be read in. Neither is more right than the other -- a payment to a card
Details is in the current account's register and in the card's, and which one
Details is wanted depends on what is being checked -- so both are offered and
Details nothing is opened until one is chosen. Choosing for the reader would be
Details wrong half the time and silent about it.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path               | Type     | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking    | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Auto      | Expense  | true        |
| Expenses:Auto:Fuel | Expense  | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries | Expense  | false       |
Given transactions are : Transaction
=FebruaryOnChecking
When the transaction is opened : TransactionSelect Vertical
| Ref | T3 |
Then panes are : PaneState
| Pane | Showing  | Account         | Active |
| 1    | Register | Assets:Checking | true   |
Then the register line selected is : RegisterSelect
| Line |
| 3    |
# T3 is the 10 February visit to the filling station. Assets:Checking is the
# register opened because it is the real account the transaction touches, and
# the checking register in date order has it third.

