# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The width of a column
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Description Dragging the divider between two headings makes a column wider or
Description narrower. The width belongs to the column, is the same in every
Description register, and is kept with the book.
Details Every register is the same shape -- the same nine columns in the same
Details order, whatever the account -- so a width learned in one of them is
Details right in all of them. Widths held per account would mean widening the
Details payee column once per account, twenty times, for one preference about
Details how long a payee name is. Nobody would do that twice.
Details
Details Kept with the book rather than with the program, for the same reason
Details every other setting is: a book is a file, and a book carried to
Details another machine should look the way it was left. See how a book is
Details stored in Books. The widths are one row per column there, keyed by the
Details column's name as this specification writes it -- not by its heading,
Details because Payment and Deposit are headed differently for different
Details account types and the width is not.
Details
Details Only a column somebody resized is recorded. A column nobody touched
Details keeps whatever width the program gives it, and that number is not
Details stated anywhere here, so changing it does not fail a test. This is the
Details one thing that distinguishes "wide by default", which is appearance,
Details from "wide because I said so", which is not.

Attributes RegisterColumnWidth
Description The width of one register column, in pixels. A width is a number
Description only because a drag produces one; see the rule above for why no
Description default width appears in any table.
| Attribute | Type    | Default |
| Column    | Text    |         |
| Width     | Integer | 0       |

$WIP
BusinessRule A column cannot be dragged away to nothing
Description A width is clamped to a minimum. A column dragged to no width at
Description all would be invisible, and a column that cannot be seen cannot be
Description dragged back, so the program would have lost it until the book was
Description edited by hand.
Examples: WidthRequested
| Requested | Stored | Notes                                   |
| 240       | 240    | Taken as asked                          |
| 20        | 20     | The minimum exactly                     |
| 8         | 20     | Clamped; the divider stops here         |
| 0         | 20     | A drag past the next column stops here  |

Attributes WidthRequested
| Attribute | Type    | Default |
| Requested | Integer | 0       |
| Stored    | Integer | 0       |
| Notes     | String  | none    |

$WIP
Scenario A column widened in one register is that wide in every register
Description The width is the column's, so moving to another account does not
Description undo it. Both of these registers have a payee column, and after
Description the drag there is one answer to how wide it is.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path             | Type       | Placeholder | Hidden |
| Assets:Checking  | Checking   | false       | false  |
| Liabilities:Visa | CreditCard | false       | false  |
When account selected : AccountSelect
| Path            |
| Assets:Checking |
When register column resized : RegisterColumnWidth Vertical
| Column | Payee |
| Width  | 240   |
When account selected : AccountSelect
| Path             |
| Liabilities:Visa |
Then register column widths are : RegisterColumnWidth CompareOnly
| Column | Width |
| Payee  | 240   |

$WIP
Scenario Only the columns somebody resized are recorded
Description The register has nine columns and one of them was dragged, so one
Description of them is written down. The other eight are whatever the program
Description makes them, which is deliberately not a number this
Description specification knows.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path            | Type     | Placeholder | Hidden |
| Assets:Checking | Checking | false       | false  |
When account selected : AccountSelect
| Path            |
| Assets:Checking |
When register column resized : RegisterColumnWidth Vertical
| Column | Memo |
| Width  | 180  |
Then recorded column widths are : RegisterColumnWidth
| Column | Width |
| Memo   | 180   |

$WIP
Scenario A width survives the book being saved and opened again
Description A preference set once and lost on exit is worse than none, because
Description it has to be set again every time and it looks like a defect.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path            | Type     | Placeholder | Hidden |
| Assets:Checking | Checking | false       | false  |
When account selected : AccountSelect
| Path            |
| Assets:Checking |
When register column resized : RegisterColumnWidth Vertical
| Column | Payee |
| Width  | 240   |
When register column resized : RegisterColumnWidth Vertical
| Column | Category |
| Width  | 300      |
When the book is saved and opened again
When account selected : AccountSelect
| Path            |
| Assets:Checking |
Then register column widths are : RegisterColumnWidth CompareOnly
| Column   | Width |
| Payee    | 240   |
| Category | 300   |

$WIP
Scenario A column dragged to nothing keeps the minimum width
Description The clamp from the rule above, through the window rather than in a
Description table of its own, because this is the case that would otherwise
Description lose a column for good.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path            | Type     | Placeholder | Hidden |
| Assets:Checking | Checking | false       | false  |
When account selected : AccountSelect
| Path            |
| Assets:Checking |
When register column resized : RegisterColumnWidth Vertical
| Column | CheckNo |
| Width  | 0       |
Then register column widths are : RegisterColumnWidth CompareOnly
| Column  | Width |
| CheckNo | 20    |
